!! 3. Why Separate Note-taking and Flashcard Creation?

Writing notes and creating flashcards are two different cognitive activities.

When writing notes, I prefer to focus entirely on understanding and organizing knowledge: exploring ideas, explaining concepts in my own words, and building connections between them.

Stopping to think about flashcard questions and answers in the middle of this process interrupts the flow of writing and forces me to switch constantly between two ways of thinking.

That's why I prefer to separate these activities.

First, focus on understanding the material and organizing your notes. Once everything is clear, move on to a dedicated flashcard creation session.

In that session, concentrate entirely on reviewing what you've written, identifying what is worth remembering, and turning those ideas into questions and answers.

This lets you stay in flow while creating flashcards, rather than interrupting your writing.

!!! From Understanding to Remembering

I prefer to understand something before trying to memorize it.

For me, learning is about connecting new information with existing knowledge, gradually turning external input into something I genuinely understand and can use.

AI can generate plenty of information, notes, and even flashcards. But generating information is not the same as internalizing it.

The important question is not how much information I can collect, but how much of it I can actually make my own.

This is why I find it useful to separate understanding from memorization, while keeping them connected through a deliberate workflow.

!! 4. How It Works

!!! 1. Write and organize knowledge in TiddlyWiki

Start by writing your notes as you normally would.

Explore ideas, collect references, and connect related information.

Focus on understanding first. You don't need to think about flashcards while writing.

!!! 2. Create flashcards with TiddlyFlashcards

Once your notes are ready, scroll down to the card editor in the current tiddler.

Focus on extracting what is worth remembering, and turn those ideas into questions and answers.

A single tiddler can contain multiple flashcards, each using a different Anki note type.

Cards can be previewed, edited, deleted, and suspended or unsuspended independently.

Each card remains associated with its original tiddler, so you can always return to the complete context when needed.

!!! 3. Sync with Anki

Sync your flashcards with Anki.

TiddlyWiki remains your primary place for authoring and managing flashcards, while TiddlyFlashcards handles syncing with Anki.

!!! 4. Review with Anki

Let Anki handle spaced repetition and review scheduling.

Focus on recalling what you've learned and gradually strengthen the memories you've chosen to retain.

!! Further Reading

If you're interested in the ideas behind this workflow, I'd highly recommend reading:

* [[20 Rules of Knowledge Formulation — SuperMemo Guru|https://supermemo.guru/wiki/20_rules_of_knowledge_formulation]] — by Piotr Wozniak; how to formulate knowledge for effective long-term learning.

